    "question": {
      ":question/id": "eb2c9a2d-f826-59f4-b2cc-96e7d9fcb994",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $5{y}^{″}=3x+y,\\,y(2)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-2$\n- **II.** $2{y}^{′}=4x+y$\n- **III.** $2{y}^{′}=3x+y,\\,y(4)=0$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $5{y}^{″}=3x+y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{y}^{′}=3x+y$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
