    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0d639531-a607-5058-8917-df400dc5f5fb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $4{y}^{″}=4x+y,\\,y(4)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=5$\n- **II.** $3{y}^{′}=5x+y,\\,y(3)=-5$\n- **III.** $3{y}^{″}=3x+y,\\,y(3)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(3)=-1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $4{y}^{″}=4x+y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(4)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=5.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{y}^{′}=5x+y$ together with the initial condition $y(3)=-5.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $3{y}^{″}=3x+y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(3)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(3)=-1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "231fe1f5-bba4-5db9-a86b-6ba03454bb64",
