      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2{y}^{′}=2x+y,\\,y(4)=-4$\n- **II.** $4{y}^{″}=2x+y,\\,y(4)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=5$\n- **III.** $3{y}^{′}=1x+y,\\,y(4)=1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{y}^{′}=2x+y$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-4.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $4{y}^{″}=2x+y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(4)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=5.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{y}^{′}=1x+y$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
